Subject: Lift the 255-argument limit on spread calls too

What this does
  Lift the 255-argument limit on spread calls too.

Why we need it
  But a call that mixes more than 255 fixed arguments with a trailing spread (`f`) is compiled with `OP_array_from` for the fixed prefix first; that opcode's register lowering still refused `argc > 255`, so the stack build ran `f(...,...a)` and the register build threw the 255-limit `InternalError` -- a sixth reachable refusal the round's scoreboard did not generate.

What it adds
  `OP_array_from`'s register case reuses `regvm_build_arg_array`, the same 255-value chunked `ROP_array_from`+`ROP_append` build the big-call path uses: at most one chunk is live, and the parser's own 65,534-argument limit bounds `argc`, so the byte count is what has to encode, not the 255 push-count.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
diff --git a/quickjs.c b/quickjs.c
index 9122b76..87ad01b 100644
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -61295,8 +61295,15 @@ static int regvm_lower_insn(JSContext *ctx, JSRegIR *ir, const uint8_t *bc,
     }
     case OP_array_from: {
         int argc = get_u16(bc + pos + 1), defs[256], a;
-        if (argc > 255)
-            return regvm_too_many_args(ctx, argc);
+        if (argc > 255) {
+            /* More than one byte of arguments: the array a spread call stages
+               its leading fixed arguments in (`f(1,...,1, ...rest)`), built
+               with the big-call path's chunking. `argc` is bounded by the
+               parser's 65,534 limit. */
+            if (regvm_build_arg_array(ctx, ir, pos, pop, argc, dst))
+                return -1;
+            return 0;
+        }
         regvm_stage_args(ir, pop, argc, 0, argc, home_of, n_values, below, defs);
